# Thresholding:
#
# This feature is used to reduce the number of logged alerts for noisy rules.
# Thresholding commands limit the number of times a particular event is logged
# during a specified time interval.
#
# The syntax is the following:
#
# threshold gen_id <gen_id>, sig_id <sig_id>, type <limit|threshold|both>, track <by_src|by_dst>, count <n>, seconds <t>
#
# event_filter gen_id <gen_id>, sig_id <sig_id>, type <limit|threshold|both>, track <by_src|by_dst>, count <n>, seconds <t>
#
# suppress gen_id <gid>, sig_id <sid>
# suppress gen_id <gid>, sig_id <sid>, track <by_src|by_dst>, ip <ip|subnet>
#
# The options are documented at https://docs.suricata.io/en/latest/configuration/global-thresholds.html
#
# Please note that thresholding can also be set inside a signature. The interaction between rule based thresholds
# and global thresholds is documented here:
# https://docs.suricata.io/en/latest/configuration/global-thresholds.html#global-thresholds-vs-rule-thresholds

# Limit to 10 alerts every 10 seconds for each source host
#threshold gen_id 0, sig_id 0, type limit, track by_src, count 10, seconds 10

# Limit to 1 alert every 10 seconds for signature with sid 2404000 per destination host
#threshold gen_id 1, sig_id 2404000, type limit, track by_dst, count 1, seconds 10

# Avoid to alert on f-secure update
# Example taken from https://blog.inliniac.net/2012/03/07/f-secure-av-updates-and-suricata-ips/
#suppress gen_id 1, sig_id 2009557, track by_src, ip 217.110.97.128/25
#suppress gen_id 1, sig_id 2012086, track by_src, ip 217.110.97.128/25
#suppress gen_id 1, sig_id 2003614, track by_src, ip 217.110.97.128/25

# ── BeaconButty: suppress Suricata stream engine noise ────────────────────────
# These are internal TCP stream tracking events (SID 2210xxx), not threat
# signatures. All Priority 3. They generate thousands of alerts per day from
# normal TCP behaviour (retransmissions, out-of-window packets, etc.) and
# contribute significantly to eve.json bloat.
suppress gen_id 1, sig_id 2210016
suppress gen_id 1, sig_id 2210020
suppress gen_id 1, sig_id 2210029
suppress gen_id 1, sig_id 2210030
suppress gen_id 1, sig_id 2210038
suppress gen_id 1, sig_id 2210042
suppress gen_id 1, sig_id 2210044
suppress gen_id 1, sig_id 2210045
suppress gen_id 1, sig_id 2210054

# QUIC decoder events (2026-11-10): sid 2231001 fired ~11k/min from a Zscaler QUIC
# tunnel, filled the 1G log2ram disk and took the box down. The UI already treats
# every "SURICATA QUIC" rule as infrastructure noise, so silence the family here.
suppress gen_id 1, sig_id 2231000
suppress gen_id 1, sig_id 2231001
suppress gen_id 1, sig_id 2231002

# ── Rate limit on every built-in "SURICATA …" decoder / app-layer event rule (2026-11-10) ──
# These fire per packet on traffic Suricata cannot parse (shape, not threat). A global
# sig_id 0 threshold is unsupported upstream, so one line per sid: at most 50 alerts per
# destination host per minute. The first 50 still log, so nothing is hidden, and real ET
# rules are untouched. Suppressed sids above are excluded (suppress wins anyway).
# Regenerate after a Suricata version upgrade (rule *set* updates do not add these):
#   grep -E '^alert .*msg:"SURICATA ' /var/lib/suricata/rules/suricata.rules | grep -oE 'sid:[0-9]+' | sort -u
threshold gen_id 1, sig_id 224008, type limit, track by_dst, count 50, seconds 60
threshold gen_id 1, sig_id 224009, type limit, track by_dst, count 50, seconds 60
threshold gen_id 1, sig_id 224010, type limit, track by_dst, count 50, seconds 60
threshold gen_id 1, sig_id 2200000, type limit, track by_dst, count 50, seconds 60
threshold gen_id 1, sig_id 2200001, type limit, track by_dst, count 50, seconds 60
threshold gen_id 1, sig_id 2200002, type limit, track by_dst, count 50, seconds 60
threshold gen_id 1, sig_id 2200003, type limit, track by_dst, count 50, seconds 60
